---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import CardList from "../components/CardList.astro";
import GlossaryTerm from "../components/GlossaryTerm.astro";
---

<PageLayout
  title="For educators"
  description="Lesson plans, workshop formats, and guidance for teaching LLMs Unplugged in your classroom or organisation."
>
  <h1>For educators</h1>

  <img
    src="/assets/images/hero-educators.avif"
    alt="Hero image: For educators"
  />

  <Callout type="info">
    <p>
      These resources are under active development. If there&rsquo;s something
      you&rsquo;d like to see, please
      <a href="/about#get-in-touch">get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout type="tip">
    <p>
      These lesson plans have been tested with high-school students and up
      (including tertiary students). They&rsquo;re also suitable across all
      subject areas (not just Computer Science/Digital Technologies).
    </p>
    <p>
      We&rsquo;re working on some slightly modified versions which work for
      younger learners as well&mdash;with the right support they can absolutely
      grasp the concepts involved. We&rsquo;ll update the lesson plans as we
      road-test them. If you&rsquo;ve got ideas or feedback, we&rsquo;d
      <a href="/about#get-in-touch">love to hear them</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Lesson plan 1: LLMs Unplugged Fundamentals</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>time</strong>: 90mins</li>
    <li><strong>for ages</strong>: 12+</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This core workshop covers the essential training-to-generation pipeline.
    Start with a brief introduction to set the scene, then move through training
    a model, generating text from it, and finally exploring what happens when
    you use a larger pre-trained model. Each step builds on the last, giving
    students a complete picture of how language models work.
  </p>

  <CardList
    lessons={["intro", "training", "generation", "pretrained-generation"]}
  />

  <h3>Suggested timing</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>00:20 <a href="/lessons/intro">Intro</a></strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>00:20 <a href="/lessons/training">Training</a></strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>00:40 <a href="/lessons/generation">Generation</a></strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong
        >01:00
        <a href="/lessons/pretrained-generation">Pre-trained model generation</a
        ></strong
      >
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>01:20 close</strong> &mdash; how has this workshop changed how you
      <em>think</em> about language models? how has this workshop changed how you
      will <em>use</em> language models?
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Notes for Fundamentals</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      this outline doesn&rsquo;t include the
      <a href="/lessons/weighted-randomness">Weighted randomness</a> lesson, but if
      your students aren&rsquo;t so familiar with that stuff then you could add it
      in before the <a href="/lessons/training">Training</a> lesson (add another 30mins)
    </li>
    <li>
      once you get to the <a href="/lessons/generation">Generation</a> lesson and
      beyond, get students to do &ldquo;dramatic readings&rdquo; as they share back
      the text their new language models have generated...
    </li>
    <li>
      if you have a bit longer, then adding the
      <a href="/lessons/sampling">Sampling</a> lesson at the end is a fun option&mdash;it
      builds on either the
      <a href="/lessons/generation">Generation</a> or the
      <a href="/lessons/pretrained-generation">Pre-trained model generation</a> work
      and shows how different parts of the &ldquo;LLM process&rdquo; can have different
      effects on the output
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Lesson plan 2: going deeper</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>time</strong>: 2&ndash;3 hours (or split across sessions)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>for ages</strong>: senior high school or particularly engaged
      groups
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For students ready to go further, this extended trajectory adds the
    &ldquo;how models understand&rdquo; topic. After covering the fundamentals,
    you explore how models can track grammatical context and how words get
    represented as numerical vectors. This path suits later-year high school
    students, computing electives, or keen beans who want to understand what
    &ldquo;<GlossaryTerm id="attention-mechanism">attention</GlossaryTerm
    >&rdquo; and &ldquo;<GlossaryTerm id="embedding">embeddings</GlossaryTerm
    >&rdquo; actually mean.
  </p>

  <CardList
    lessons={[
      "intro",
      "training",
      "generation",
      "pretrained-generation",
      "context-columns",
      "word-embeddings",
    ]}
  />

  <h3>What these additions cover</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/lessons/context-columns">Context columns</a></strong>
      extends the basic bigram model with extra columns that track grammatical categories
      (is the previous word a verb? a pronoun? a preposition?). This is a hand-crafted
      version of what transformer &ldquo;attention&rdquo; learns automatically&mdash;the
      idea that <em>type</em> of context matters, not just <em
        >which specific word</em
      > came before.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/lessons/word-embeddings">Word embeddings</a></strong>
      turns each word&rsquo;s row in the model into a numerical vector and measures
      similarities between words. Words that behave similarly in the training text
      end up close together. This is the foundation of how modern LLMs represent meaning&mdash;and
      students can calculate it by hand.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why split the trajectory?</h3>

  <p>
    The fundamentals work for any audience and require only 90 minutes. The
    &ldquo;understanding&rdquo; lessons require more time and comfort with
    abstraction, but they connect directly to concepts students will encounter
    in any deeper study of AI: attention mechanisms, embeddings, vector
    similarity. Running them as a second session (or a follow-up for interested
    students) keeps the core workshop accessible while offering a clear path
    forward.
  </p>

  <h2>Lesson plan 3: controlling output</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>time</strong>: 30mins (as an add-on)</li>
    <li><strong>for ages</strong>: 14+</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Once students can generate text, a natural question is: &ldquo;How do you
    make it more or less creative?&rdquo; The sampling lesson shows how
    temperature and truncation strategies change the character of output without
    changing the model itself. This is a quick add-on to either the fundamentals
    or the deeper trajectory.
  </p>

  <CardList lessons={["sampling"]} />

  <p>This lesson explains:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>temperature</strong>: how dividing counts by a temperature value
      flattens or sharpens the distribution, making surprising words more or
      less likely
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>truncation</strong>: strategies like greedy selection, no-repeat,
      or even haiku constraints that narrow which words are eligible before
      sampling
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Students discover that &ldquo;creativity&rdquo; in AI comes from two
    controls: adjusting probability distributions and filtering which tokens to
    consider. The same model can produce cautious prose or wild poetry just by
    tweaking these parameters.
  </p>

  <h2>Adaptation and data</h2>

  <p>
    For classes focused on data science, ethics, or media literacy, the
    &ldquo;Adaptation and data&rdquo; topic explores what happens when models
    train on their own output.
  </p>

  <CardList topics={["adaptation-and-data"]} />

  <p>
    The <a href="/lessons/synthetic-data">Synthetic data</a> lesson is particularly
    effective for discussions about:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>AI-generated content flooding the internet</li>
    <li>model collapse and why training data quality matters</li>
    <li>the difference between human-written and AI-generated text</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This works well as a standalone activity after students have done basic
    training and generation, or as part of a broader unit on AI ethics and media
    literacy.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
